  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163666",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16+16-0 \\\\  & =32\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =50-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $3\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-3=2\\,m/s.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
